he user has access to the CoreID Admin Console.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the text is not very large, making it slightly difficult to read.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a user interface for CoreID, an identity verification system. The user is currently signed in as A. Rao, who has L1 support and password reset admin privileges.\n\n**Summary:**\nThe image displays a user interface for CoreID, an identity verification system, with the user signed in as A. Rao, who has L1 support and password reset admin privileges.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n* The application is CoreID, an identity verification system.\n* The current screen is the \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" page.\n* The overall layout is a white background with black text and blue checkmarks.\n* The page has a title bar with the user's name and role, and a navigation menu on the left side.\n* The main content area displays a list of options, including \"Employee ID matches ticket\", \"Manager name matches ticket\", and \"Department matches record\".\n* There are two blue checkmarks next to \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n* The page also includes a message at the bottom indicating that 2 out of 4 confirmed matches are required to proceed.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n* None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n* Category: Unknown\n* Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n* OBSERVED FACTS:\n\t+ The user is signed in as A. Rao.\n\t+ The user has L1 support and password reset admin privileges.\n\t+ The page displays a list of options for identity verification.\n\t+ There are two blue checkmarks next to \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n* INFERENCE:\n\t+ The user is attempting to verify their identity using CoreID.\n\t+ The system is checking for matches between the employee ID, manager name, and department record.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, with no missing or unclear text. The layout and design of the page are consistent with a typical user interface.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one.\n\n* **Application**: The application is CoreID, an admin console.\n* **Current Screen**: The current screen is the password reset page.\n* **Layout and UI Elements**: The page has a white background with black text and a blue button. There are two fields: one for the temporary password and another for the new password.\n* **Buttons and Menus**: There is a \"Generate Temporary Password\" button below the fields.\n* **Notifications**: There are no notifications visible on the page.\n* **Highlighted Areas**: The temporary password field is highlighted in blue.\n* **Unu